WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.000
Authorization failures occur when an application does not reliably verify whether the

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.940
authenticated user is allowed to perform a specific action or access a specific payment

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:11.040
record.

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:16.380
Login alone is not enough; every sensitive request needs server-side authorization.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:21.080
Privilege escalation can give a lower-privileged user administrative capabilities or

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.340
access to records belonging to another account.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:29.680
In payment environments this can expose transaction history, refunds, customer data,

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:31.740
or administrative functions.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:37.000
Access control bypasses often appear in APIs, hidden routes, disabled buttons,

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:40.980
direct URL access, or client-controlled role values.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.680
If the server trusts client-side decisions,

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.700
attackers can often call the underlying function directly.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:52.360
Insecure direct object references happen when identifiers such as order IDs, customer IDs,

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:53.940
invoice IDs,

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.000
or payment record references are exposed without checking ownership or role-based

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:02.700
permission. Apply least privilege to users, services, and administrators.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:06.120
Use role-based access control with explicit permission checks.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:10.100
Enforce authorization on the server for every sensitive object and action.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.840
Selected PCI DSS mappings for this topic include Req.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:19.180
7.1 - Restrict access to system components and cardholder data; Req.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:26.760
7.2 - Role-based access control; Req. 8.2 - User authentication management.
